                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Hack Run ZERO is a strange little adventure game that is almost entirely built around pretending I am sitting at an old computer terminal. Instead of walking around a normal game world, I have to type commands into a DOS and Unix style interface and work my way through different computer accounts. The story starts with a ransom message involving someone close to me, and I have to follow the kidnapper's instructions while digging deeper into a secretive organisation. It is a very unusual way of telling a story, and I like that the game commits fully to the idea rather than adding a normal adventure section on top.\n\nThe actual gameplay is mostly about exploring the systems and figuring out what commands I can use. I move between accounts, read messages and files, search for information and use what I discover to work out where I need to go next. There are over 70 levels, so there is a lot more to the game than the basic idea might suggest. I like the feeling of gradually understanding how the systems work because the game does not hand me a traditional map or quest marker. I have to pay attention and remember what I find.\n\nThe story is probably the main reason I keep going. As I access more accounts, I start uncovering information about the organisation and the people connected to it. The computer interface makes even simple pieces of information feel more important because I have to actively search for them. It creates a nice feeling of investigating something that I probably should not be seeing. I also like that the game gives me hints and commands to help when I get completely stuck, so I am not necessarily forced to search online for every difficult section.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that the interface can become frustrating. Typing commands is part of the whole point, but it also means that I sometimes spend more time trying to work out the correct command than thinking about the actual puzzle. There are moments where the game expects me to understand its particular way of doing things, and that can make progress feel less natural. The lack of normal graphics is also going to be a major limitation for me if I am looking for something visually interesting. Almost everything depends on text, menus and my imagination.\n\nI also think the game works better when I play it in shorter sessions. There is a lot of information to remember, and because the entire game is based around investigating different accounts, I can start losing track of names and clues if I try to rush through too much at once. The older computer interface is part of the charm, but it also makes the game feel dated. I do not mind that as much because the whole concept is intentionally old school, but it means I have to be in the right mood for a text based investigation rather than expecting something that feels like a modern hacking game.\n\nWhat I appreciate most is that Hack Run ZERO does not really need to pretend that I am doing real hacking. It is essentially a puzzle adventure using the language and appearance of old computer systems, and that makes it much more approachable than an actual technical simulation would be. There are also 72 Steam achievements, which gives me plenty of extra things to discover while working through the game. The fact that it is free makes the unusual design easier to recommend too, especially because there is a substantial amount of content here for such a small download.\n\nOverall, Hack Run ZERO is a clever and unusual adventure that I enjoyed mainly because it does something very different with storytelling. I like exploring the fake computer systems, finding hidden information and slowly piecing together what is actually happening. The command based gameplay can be awkward and the lack of visual variety will not suit everyone, but I think the strange presentation is also what gives it most of its personality. It is not a game I would recommend for action or traditional puzzle gameplay, but if I want a slow mystery where I have to actually investigate instead of simply following objectives, this is a pretty interesting little experience.\n\nRating: 6.7/10\n",
